Subject: [PATCH] media: capture device counts from the profile

The reference M4 Max enumerates one audioinput, one videoinput and one
audiooutput. Our build enumerated an audiooutput and nothing else: a MacBook
Pro with no microphone and no camera, which is not a MacBook Pro. It is a
property of the headless host, and it is readable from any page with no
permission — enumerateDevices() returns the counts before it returns anything
identifying.

Injected where every enumeration result lands, not at the accessor. The same
snapshot MediaDevicesManager::DevicesEnumerated feeds into the cache also
drives device-change events and getUserMedia's device selection, so a device
added at enumerateDevices() would be one the rest of the browser does not
believe in — the count would agree with the claimed device while the behaviour
around it disagreed.

The profile sets a floor, not an exact count. If the host genuinely has capture
hardware those devices are real and are kept, and the patch only tops the list
up to the claimed number. Removing real devices to hit an exact count would
break capture on a machine that can actually capture, to fix a number.

Identifiers are opaque on purpose. MediaDevicesDispatcherHost replaces every
device_id with a per-origin HMAC before it reaches a renderer, so the raw string
is never observable and does not need to imitate any platform's naming
convention. "default" and "communications" are deliberately avoided: both are
special-cased downstream and mean something specific.

Counts rather than booleans because a machine can have several cameras, bounded
at 16 so that a malformed profile cannot make the browser synthesise an
unbounded device list.

Known limit, not fixed here. These devices enumerate but cannot capture: a page
that is granted permission and calls getUserMedia gets an error where a real Mac
gets a stream. That is reachable only after a permission prompt, which is a
different threat model from the unprompted enumeration this fixes, but it is a
real residual and belongs in the ledger rather than in silence. Wiring the
synthetic devices to a capture backend is the follow-up.

Ledger: media.device-counts.
diff --git a/base/apostate/profile.cc b/base/apostate/profile.cc
index cd466fc..54ca7a3 100644
--- a/base/apostate/profile.cc
+++ b/base/apostate/profile.cc
@@ -196,6 +196,26 @@ Profile* Profile::ParseOrNull(const std::string& encoded) {
     }
   }
 
+  if (const DictValue* media = dict->FindDict("media")) {
+    // A machine can have several cameras, so these are counts rather than
+    // booleans. The ceiling is arbitrary but finite: it exists so a malformed
+    // profile cannot make the browser synthesise an unbounded device list.
+    for (const auto& [key, sink] :
+         {std::pair<const char*, std::optional<int>*>{
+              "audioinput_count", &profile->media_audioinput_count_},
+          std::pair<const char*, std::optional<int>*>{
+              "videoinput_count", &profile->media_videoinput_count_}}) {
+      if (std::optional<int> v = media->FindInt(key)) {
+        if (*v >= 0 && *v <= 16) {
+          *sink = *v;
+        } else {
+          LOG(ERROR) << "apostate: media." << key << " must be in [0, 16]; "
+                     << "ignoring";
+        }
+      }
+    }
+  }
+
   if (const DictValue* locale = dict->FindDict("locale")) {
     if (const std::string* v = locale->FindString("timezone")) {
       profile->timezone_ = *v;
